When `handler` is not registered for the given `type`, `indexOf` returns `-1`. Calling `.splice(-1, 1)` tells JavaScript to "remove 1 element starting from the second-to-last position," which incorrectly removes the **last** element (a valid handler that should remain).

The fix is to check the index before calling splice:Let me verify the change was applied correctly:## Cause

In `src/index.ts:82-91`, the `off` method removes a handler by calling:

```ts
handlers.splice(handlers.indexOf(handler), 1);
```

When the handler was never registered for the given `type`, `indexOf` returns `-1`. Calling `[...].splice(-1, 1)` means "remove 1 element starting at the second-to-last position" — it deletes the **last** element (a valid handler) instead of doing nothing.

## Fix

Added an explicit check for `index !== -1` before calling `splice`:

```ts
if (handler) {
    const index = handlers.indexOf(handler);
    if (index !== -1) {
        handlers.splice(index, 1);
    }
}
```

